    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-221734",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a\\in Z,$ if $4∣a,$ then $12∣a.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ {{field-6}}.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n4{{field-7}}8\\,\\text{and}\\,12{{field-8}}8.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "worked_solution": "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,4∣a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,12∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(4∣a)\\,∧\\,(12∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n4∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,12∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=8$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=8.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n4∣8\\,\\text{and}\\,12∤8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=8$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
